"Structure-preserving conservative phase-amplitude PINNs for semiclassical Schrödinger-type equations - ScienceDirect (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0377042726002505)\nciteturn0search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; In this paper, we propose conservative phase–amplitude physics–informed neural networks (CPA–PINNs) for semiclassical Schrödinger–type equations in two space dimensions.The complex wavefield is represented through a WKB inspired phase–amplitude ansatz that exposes hydrodynamic quantities at the network level and these are used to build structure preserving loss terms that enforce approximate continuity and conservation of mass and energy in addition to fitting the strong form residual and available data. ... Robustness tests with additive complex noise up to 5% and 10% show that CPA–PINNs maintain stable parameter recovery and small drift in the discrete invariants even under significant data corruption. ... ### A structure-preserving PINN with embedded periodic boundary layer and adaptively enforced initial conditions for geometric flows\n\n## Journal of Computational and Applied Mathematics\n\nVolume 485, 15 October 2026, 117607\n\n# Structure-preserving conservative phase-amplitude PINNs for semiclassical Schrödinger-type equations\n\nhttps://doi.org/10.1016/j.cam.2026.117607Get rights and content\n\n## Abstract\n\nIn this paper, we propose conservative phase–amplitude physics–informed neural networks (CPA–PINNs) for semiclassical Schrödinger–type equations in two space dimensions. The complex wavefield is represented through a WKB inspired phase–amplitude ansatz that exposes hydrodynamic quantities at the network level and these are used to build structure preserving loss terms that enforce approximate continuity and conservation of mass and energy in addition to fitting the strong form residual and available data. This design enhances control over the hydrodynamic invariants while remaining mesh free, easy to implement on standard deep learning platforms, and well suited for problems where classical high order solvers are expensive or where data are sparse and noisy. Numerical experiments are performed on a forward linear plane–wave benchmark, on the forward propagation of a moving oblique dark soliton in the defocusing nonlinear Schrödinger equation (NLS), and on an inverse problem that recovers the semiclassical parameter and the defocusing strength in a strongly nonlinear regime. The results demonstrate accurate reconstruction of the wavefield together with faithful tracking of the hydrodynamic diagnostics. Robustness tests with additive complex noise up to 5% and 10% show that CPA–PINNs maintain stable parameter recovery and small drift in the discrete invariants even under significant data corruption. These findings indicate that CPA–PINNs provide an effective machine learning framework for structure preserving approximation and inversion of semiclassical Schrödinger–type equations.\n\n## Introduction\n\nPartial differential equations (PDEs) provide a fundamental language for modeling the evolution of complex systems in physics, engineering, and applied sciences, ranging from quantum dynamics and nonlinear optics to fluid mechanics and plasma physics [1], [2], [3]. In several cases, conservation constraints are enforced only at a few discrete time levels or through global integral quantities, which provides limited control of the local continuity residual in space–time. Moreover, current structure preserving PINNs for dispersive equations typically operate on the raw complex field without separating the rapidly oscillatory phase from the slowly varying amplitude. This makes it difficult to maintain phase fidelity when ϵ is small and to use mass and energy invariants as sensitive probes of numerical accuracy. Finally, most existing frameworks are tailored either to forward simulation or to simple inverse tasks and have not been systematically tested in semiclassical regimes where parameter identification must be performed under strong oscillations and where conservation diagnostics are crucial. Taken together, these limitations motivate the development of a deep learning methodology that couples phase–amplitude representations with explicit hydrodynamic conservation penalties, so that semiclassical STE can be learned in a way that remains faithful to both the governing equation and its intrinsic invariant structure.\n\nBuilding on this perspective, we introduce a conservative phase–amplitude PINNs framework, referred to as CPA–PINNs, that is specifically tailored to semiclassical STE and their hydrodynamic invariants. The starting point is a WKB style representation of the complex wavefield in which a real valued phase encodes the rapid semiclassical oscillations while a complex amplitude captures the slower modulation of the envelope. The distinctive feature of CPA–PINNs is that this phase–amplitude representation is used to expose the hydrodynamic variables associated with the wavefield inside the network, which in turn allows us to embed explicit penalties on local continuity balance and on the global evolution of mass and Hamiltonian energy. In this way, the surrogate is driven not only to solve the governing equation and match initial or boundary observations but also to keep the continuity residual small and to maintain near invariant trajectories for the conserved quantities over the entire training horizon. The resulting framework is meshfree and combines the flexibility of PINNs with a built in structure–preserving mechanism and is designed to operate in both forward and inverse settings where accurate tracking of semiclassical density and flux diagnostics is essential.\n\nThe remainder of the manuscript is organized as follows. Section 2 introduces the proposed CPA-PINNs framework. Section 3 applies CPA-PINNs to a forward 2D semiclassical plane wave with constant potential. Section 4 turns to a forward 2D defocusing NLS configuration with a moving oblique dark soliton and examines the accuracy and structure preserving behaviour of CPA-PINNs under a stagewise training strategy in a genuinely nonlinear regime. Section 5 formulates an inverse problem for the same dark soliton. Section 6 studies the robustness of the framework in both forward and inverse settings. Finally, Section 7 summarizes the main findings and outlines several directions for future work.\n\n## Conservative phase–amplitude PINNs (CPA–PINNs)\n\nIn this section, we present our CPA-PINNs for semiclassical two-dimensional STE. The key idea is to parametrize the complex wave field through a phase-amplitude decomposition and to train a neural surrogate that is simultaneously (i) physics-informed, in the sense that it enforces the strong form of the governing equation together with the prescribed initial and boundary conditions, and (ii) structure-preserving, in the sense that it approximately respects the underlying conservation laws such\n\n## Forward 2D semiclassical plane-wave problem\n\nIn this section, we apply the proposed CPA-PINNs framework to a forward benchmark for a two dimensional semiclassical plane wave solution of a linear STE with constant potential. This configuration probes the method on a genuinely space-time domain, in which the phase depends on both spatial directions and time and the governing residual couples second order spatial derivatives with oscillatory temporal dynamics. Beyond testing approximation accuracy, the plane wave regime provides a clean\n## Conclusion and future perspectives\n\nThis work introduced a conservative phase–amplitude physics–informed neural network framework, referred to as CPA–PINNs, for semiclassical two–dimensional Schrödinger–type equations. The central design principle is to embed the hydrodynamic structure of the governing equation directly into the learning objective. To this end, the complex wavefield is represented through a phase–amplitude ansatz that exposes the mass density {\\rho }_{\\Theta }, the probability current {\\mathbf{J}}_{\\Theta } and the Hamiltonian density {h}_{\\Theta } at the\n\n\n## References (41)\n\n  * T.J. Bridges et al.\n\n### Multi-symplectic integrators: numerical schemes for hamiltonian PDEs\n\n### Phys. Lett. A\n\n(2001)\n\n  * S.K. Lele\n\n### Compact finite difference schemes with spectral-like resolution\n\n### J. Comput. Phys.\n\n(1992)\n\n  * S. Jin et al.\n\n### Gaussian wave packet transform based numerical scheme for the semiclassical Schrödinger equation with random inputs\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nComputation of the Time-Dependent Dirac Equation with Physics-Informed Neural Networks (https://arxiv.org/abs/2204.02959)\nciteturn0academia12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4.5 years ago; Title: Computation of the Time-Dependent Dirac Equation with Physics-Informed Neural NetworksAuthors: Emmanuel Lorin, Xu Yang ... We propose to compute the time-dependent Dirac equation using physics-informed neural networks (PINNs), a new powerful tool in scientific machine learning avoiding the use of approximate derivatives of differential operators.\nTitle: Computation of the Time-Dependent Dirac Equation with Physics-Informed Neural Networks\nAuthors: Emmanuel Lorin, Xu Yang\nDate: Wed Apr  6 17:49:04 2022\n\nWe propose to compute the time-dependent Dirac equation using physics-informed neural networks (PINNs), a new powerful tool in scientific machine learning avoiding the use of approximate derivatives of differential operators. PINNs search solutions in the form of parameterized (deep) neural networks, whose derivatives (in time and space) are performed by automatic differentiation. The computational cost comes from the need to solve high-dimensional optimization problems using stochastic gradient methods and train the network with a large number of points. Specifically, we derive PINNs-based algorithms and present some key fundamental properties of these algorithms when applied to the Dirac equations in different physical frameworks.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nWKB-PINNs: Physics-informed neural networks for highly oscillatory Schrödinger–type equations - ScienceDirect (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0167278926002903)\nciteturn0search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 weeks ago; We study a phase–amplitude physics-informed neural network (PINN) architecture, termed ‘Wentzel–Kramers–Brillouin (WKB)-PINNs, for Schrödinger-type equations (STEs) whose solutions are highly oscillatory in small-wavelength regimes.The WKB-PINNs framework enlarges the applicability of PINNs for Schrödinger-type PDEs by introducing a phase–amplitude representation that separates a learned real phase from a complex amplitude. ... In this setting, we regard the network as a parametric surrogate for the solution field as a function approximator which reads\\tilde{v}:={v}_{\\text{PINN}}=\\mathcal{N}(\\varkappa ,y,t;\\Theta ),where v_{PINN} denotes the network’s prediction, \\mathcal{N} specifies the architecture (layers, activations, and connectivity), and Θ collects all trainable parameters.\n\n## Physica D: Nonlinear Phenomena\n\nDate: November 2026\n\nArticle: 135394\n\nVolume: Volume 497\n\nView accessibility information\n\n## Published by: Elsevier\n\n### Published by\n\nShow more\n\nResearch article\n\n# WKB-PINNs: Physics-informed neural networks for highly oscillatory Schrödinger–type equations\n\nAuthor links open overlay panel Jinxia Cen ^{a}, Himanshu Kumar Dwivedi ^{b}, Shubham Kumar ^{b}, Rajeev ^{b}, Shengda Zeng ^{c}\n\n10.1016/j.physd.2026.135394\n\n## Abstract\n\nWe study a phase–amplitude physics-informed neural network (PINN) architecture, termed ‘Wentzel–Kramers–Brillouin (WKB)-PINNs, for Schrödinger-type equations (STEs) whose solutions are highly oscillatory in small-wavelength regimes. The WKB-PINNs framework enlarges the applicability of PINNs for Schrödinger-type PDEs by introducing a phase–amplitude representation that separates a learned real phase from a complex amplitude. By leveraging the WKB factorization, we formulate an eikonal-regularized objective that encodes ray geometry as an inductive bias and stabilizes training. We further introduce parameterized activation functions (PAFs) with trainable slope and scale to mitigate spectral bias and improve residual conditioning, reducing the burden of learning rapid oscillations directly and limiting the transfer of phase errors into the amplitude. The WKB-PINNs framework is mesh-free and easy to implement, and it applies to the stationary and time-dependent Schrödinger settings considered in this work without requiring repeated PDE solves. We validate the approach on forward problems and inverse tasks across multiple dimensions and representative potentials. Robustness is assessed in a controlled observation model by injecting additive measurement noise (up to 5% and 10%) into complex-valued field observations, and we observe stable accuracy under these perturbations.\n\n## Keywords\n\nSchrödinger-type equations\n\n;\n\nPhysics-informed neural networks\n\n;\n\nDeep learning\n\n;\n\nWKB\n\n## MSC\n\n65M32\n\n;\n\n35Q41\n\n;\n\n35R30\n\n;\n\n81Q20\n\n;\n\n68T07\n\n## Introduction\n\nPartial differential equations (PDEs) model complex phenomena across physics, engineering, mechanics, chemistry, and finance [1], [2]. In parallel with these, the Wentzel–Kramers–Brillouin (WKB) approximation motivates a phase–amplitude decomposition that factors out dominant oscillations and exposes a smoother amplitude, which in turn inspires more learnable representations for neural surrogates. Prominent implementations include the WKB one step scheme [28] and the phase function methodology developed in [29], [30]. After extracting the leading oscillatory factor, the transformed problem admits a smooth solution or oscillations with rapidly decreasing amplitude as ϵ → 0, which enables accurate computation on relatively coarse grids. In particular, WKB marching and related phase–amplitude approaches can be asymptotically accurate in regimes where their assumptions hold, and they provide valuable guidance for constructing phase-aware representations. This phase and amplitude perspective captures the underlying ray geometry and provides analytical and algorithmic guidance throughout the semiclassical regime.\n\nIn recent years, data-driven approaches for PDEs have advanced rapidly. Unlike classical discretizations that rely on meshes and explicit time stepping, PINNs are mesh free, straightforward to implement, and adaptable across PDE models. With automatic differentiation, differential operators are evaluated inside the network, which removes the need for adjoint derivations and enables a unified treatment of data and physics in both forward and inverse tasks. In moderate-to-high dimensions, randomized collocation and neural approximation can reduce the reliance on full tensor-product grids and can be advantageous in certain settings [42]. These advantages complement traditional solvers, and our aim is to improve PINN trainability and accuracy for small-wavelength (highly oscillatory) Schrödinger-type problems through a phase aware design. A direct application of standard PINNs to Eq. (1) is ineffective when ϵ is small because the solution has wavelength O(ϵ) oscillations, the strong form residual is stiff, the required collocation density per wavelength increases, and phase errors contaminate amplitude learning, leading to phase–amplitude entanglement. Learning mechanisms such as PhaseDNN style frequency demodulation [43], neural eikonal solvers consistent with geometric optics [44], and architectures with explicit high frequency features [45], [46] help represent oscillations or estimate a phase field, yet they do not by themselves enforce the full Schrödinger-type dynamics with boundary and initial data or resolve the conditioning issues at small ϵ. They therefore complement rather than replace a phase aware design that explicitly separates phase and amplitude and regularizes the phase to reflect geometric optics. These considerations motivate the development of a deep learning methodology that exploits the intrinsic structure of Eq. (1) to compute accurate solutions.\n\nGuided by these characteristics and the broad uptake of PINNs, we introduce a PINN-based framework tailored to Eq. (1). A direct application of standard PINNs is ineffective when ϵ is small, as discussed earlier. We therefore combine the WKB approximation with PINNs and propose WKB-PINNs tailored to highly oscillatory Schrödinger-type PDEs in small-wavelength regimes. The field is factored into a phase and an amplitude, aligning the representation with geometric optics. The learned phase is further regularized with an eikonal term so that it reflects the ray geometry and the residual becomes better conditioned. This phase-aware design mitigates spectral bias, reduces the required collocation density per wavelength, and limits leakage of phase errors into amplitude learning. In practice, the method improves stability and accuracy for small ϵ and is effective for forward problems and inverse parameter identification tasks under the observation models considered here.\n\n### The WKB-PINNs framework\n\nIn this section, we introduce WKB-PINNs for the 2D-STE. We develop a phase-aware variant of PINNs that combines a phase–amplitude factorization for highly oscillatory (small-wavelength) regimes, an eikonal-regularized training objective, and parameterized activation functions. 2.1 Phase–amplitude factorization: Phase and amplitude networks, 2.2 Eikonal regularized loss, and 2.3 describe these core components.\n\n### Forward Airy equation problem\n\nIn this section, we apply the WKB-PINNs framework to the Airy equation on a bounded interval, a standard oscillatory benchmark in a small-wavelength regime that provides a closed-form reference for quantitative evaluation and for assessing phase–amplitude PINN training behavior.\n\n### Forward exponential potential model problem\n\nIn this section, we consider a two dimensional stationary benchmark with an exponential potential that produces highly oscillatory solutions for small ϵ. The goal here is to stress-test the phase–amplitude representation and the eikonal-regularized training objective in a heterogeneous medium, while retaining a closed-form exact solution for rigorous, quantitative error assessment.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSpectral Informed Neural Network: An Efficient and Low-Memory PINN (https://arxiv.org/abs/2408.16414)\nciteturn0academia13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.1 years ago; Title: Spectral Informed Neural Network: An Efficient and Low-Memory PINN ... In this paper, we focus on removing the automatic differentiation of the spatial derivatives and propose a spectral-based neural network that substitutes the differential operator with a multiplication.\nTitle: Spectral Informed Neural Network: An Efficient and Low-Memory PINN\nAuthors: Tianchi Yu, Yiming Qi, Ivan Oseledets, Shiyi Chen\nDate: Thu Aug 29 10:21:00 2024\n\nWith growing investigations into solving partial differential equations by physics-informed neural networks (PINNs), more accurate and efficient PINNs are required to meet the practical demands of scientific computing. One bottleneck of current PINNs is computing the high-order derivatives via automatic differentiation which often necessitates substantial computing resources. In this paper, we focus on removing the automatic differentiation of the spatial derivatives and propose a spectral-based neural network that substitutes the differential operator with a multiplication. Compared to the PINNs, our approach requires lower memory and shorter training time. Thanks to the exponential convergence of the spectral basis, our approach is more accurate. Moreover, to handle the different situations between physics domain and spectral domain, we provide two strategies to train networks by their spectral information. Through a series of comprehensive experiments, We validate the aforementioned merits of our proposed network.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA fourth-order compact time-splitting Fourier pseudospectral method for the Dirac equation (https://arxiv.org/abs/1711.07193)\nciteturn0academia14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8.9 years ago; Title: A fourth-order compact time-splitting Fourier pseudospectral method for the Dirac equation ... We propose a new fourth-order compact time-splitting ($S_\\text{4c}$) Fourier pseudospectral method for the Dirac equation by splitting the Dirac equation into two parts together with using the double commutator between them to integrate the Dirac equation at each time interval.\nTitle: A fourth-order compact time-splitting Fourier pseudospectral method for the Dirac equation\nAuthors: Weizhu Bao, Jia Yin\nDate: Mon Nov 20 08:28:12 2017\n\nWe propose a new fourth-order compact time-splitting ($S_\\text{4c}$) Fourier pseudospectral method for the Dirac equation by splitting the Dirac equation into two parts together with using the double commutator between them to integrate the Dirac equation at each time interval. The method is explicit, fourth-order in time and spectral order in space. It is unconditional stable and conserves the total density in the discretized level. It is called a compact time-splitting method since, at each time step, the number of sub-steps in $S_\\text{4c}$ is much less than those of the standard fourth-order splitting method and the fourth-order partitioned Runge-Kutta splitting method. Comparison among $S_\\text{4c}$ and many other existing time-splitting methods for the Dirac equation are carried out in terms of accuracy and efficiency as well as long time behavior. Numerical results demonstrate the advantage in terms of efficiency and accuracy of the proposed $S_\\text{4c}$. Finally we report the spatial/temporal resolutions of $S_\\text{4c}$ for the Dirac equation in different parameter regimes including the nonrelativistic limit regime, the semiclassical limit regime, and the simultaneously nonrelativisic and massless limit regime.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nImproved uniform error bounds on time-splitting methods for the long-time dynamics of the Dirac equation with small potentials (https://arxiv.org/abs/2112.03616)\nciteturn0academia15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4.8 years ago; We begin with a semi-discritization of the Dirac equation in time by a time-splitting method, and then followed by a full-discretization in space by the Fourier pseudospectral method.Employing the unitary flow property of the second-order time-splitting method for the Dirac equation, we prove uniform error bounds at $C(t)\\tau^2$ and $C(t)(h^m+\\tau^2)$ for the semi-discretization and full-discretization, respectively, for any time $t\\in[0,T_\\varepsilon]$ with $T_\\varepsilon = T/\\varepsilon$ for $T > 0$, which are uniformly for $\\varepsilon \\in (0, 1]$, where $\\tau$ is the time step, $h$ is the mesh size, $m\\geq 2$ depends on the regularity of the solution, and $C(t) = C_0 + C_1\\varepsilon t\\le C_0+C_1T$ grows at most linearly with respect to $t$ with $C_0\\ge0$ and $C_1>0$ two constants independent of $t$, $h$, $\\tau$ and $\\varepsilon$.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nUniform Error Bounds of Time-Splitting Methods for the Nonlinear Dirac Equation in the Nonrelativistic Regime without Magnetic Potential | SIAM Journal on Numerical Analysis (https://epubs.siam.org/doi/abs/10.1137/19M1271828)\nciteturn0search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5.5 years ago; Crawled: 2.4 years ago; # Uniform Error Bounds of Time-Splitting Methods for the Nonlinear Dirac Equation in the Nonrelativistic Regime